  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n   We'll implement a hybrid strategy combining coordinate sampling with gradient-based optimization. The core idea is to:\n   - Maintain several sequence templates (positive real coefficients) with varying lengths\n   - Use their average as a base representation for gradient updates\n   - Regularly perturb these templates using orthogonal transformations while preserving sum constraints\n   - This approach alternates between stable optimization phases and intensive exploration phases\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   Representation: Multiset {v\u2081, v\u2082, ..., vk} of normalized sequences\n   Objective: Minimize 2*n*||v*v||\u209a / (sum(v))\u00b2\n   Constraints:\n     - sum(v) >= 0.01 after normalization\n     - non-negative terms\n     - normalized to simplex after each update\n\n   We'll use:\n   - Principal Axis Sampling: Initialize sequences along gradient-aligned axes\n   - Gradient Transport (GT) phase: Send mass between optimized sequences using coordinates\n   - Morphological Refinement (MR) phase: Apply morphing operations to escape local minima\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   Phase I: Initialization\n     - Set n_base = [min_length, average_attempted_length, max_length] from parent or default\n     - Generate initial sequences: Uniform and gradient-aligned\n     - Normalize each sequence while maintaining orthogonality\n\n   Phase II: Principal Axis Sampling\n     - Sample orthogonal vectors along coordinate axes\n     - For each vector:\n         Use templated correlation\n         Apply gradient descent to fixed-sum optimization\n         Track best_fit based on minimal objective\n\n   Phase III: Gradient Transport\n     - Create process vectors (conjugate gradients)\n     - For each time interval:\n         Calculate transport values along axes\n         Update sequences using coordinate transport\n         Check for objective improvement at 1-second marks\n\n   Phase IV: Morphological Refinement\n     - Apply diagonal perturbations to several best sequences\n     - Use nested temperature schedules\n     - High-precision relaxation with multiple quadrants\n\n   Phase V: Final Mix and Evaluation\n     - Combine all successful templates into a master schedule\n     - Apply final multi-vector Lambert optimization\n     - Verify time remaining for gradient ascent refinement\n\n4. Search Choices and Hyperparameter Tuning:\n   - Primary hyperparameters: n_active = 15, n_vectors = 25\n   - Secondary parameters: alpha = [0.01, 0.05, 0.1]\n   - Influence controls: \n       beta_min = 0.2, beta_max = 0.8 scaling factors\n       delta = 0.005 step size in Lambert space\n       gamma = 0.95 temperature decay factor\n\n   Optimization triggers:\n     - Length jump: When current sequences > 200 or < 100\n     - Stalling: When improvement rate < 0.1% for 15 cycles\n     - Blend ratio: 0.4-0.6 between samples/optimizations\n\n5. Time Budget and Convergence Management:\n   - Initial sampling: 20% cycle time (300s for n_active=15)\n   - Principal axis operation: 25% (400s)\n   - Gradient transport: 15% (240s) per weighting cycle (used every 5 cycles)\n   - Morphological refinement: 10% (150s) at critical stalling points\n\n   Time monitoring:\n     - Check at 1, 3, 5, 8, 12, 15 minute marks\n     - Use exponential moving average for improvement indicators\n     - Final checkpoint at 190 seconds (half remaining time)\n\n6. Output and Validation:\n   - Return master_sequence = current_best_rescaled\n   - Validate: evaluate_sequence(master_sequence \u2192 float)\n   - If valid and significant improvement (score < best_prev - 0.005), save\n   - Fall back: Uniform distribution of length 500 (score ~ 2.5)\n\n   The implementation will:\n     - Normalize all inputs\n     - Track gradients with built-in derivative estimation\n     - Use cross-dimensional locking effectively\n     - Ensure all updates maintain non-negative and simplex-normalization properties",
